\details{
\subsection{Features}{

The function reads features of these types: \code{CDS}, \code{rRNA}, \code{misc_RNA},
\code{mRNA}, \code{misc_feature}, and \code{gene}. If a record has none of them, the
function makes one feature for the full record. If a record has more than
one feature for the same marker (for example, a \code{gene} and a \code{CDS}), the
function keeps the most specific one.

To find features without a marker, use \code{dplyr::filter(features, is.na(marker))}. Then add a row or change a regular expression in the
dictionary.
}

\subsection{Taxonomic ranks}{

The ranks come from the NCBI Taxonomy data that \code{\link[=gb_download]{gb_download()}}
saved. The \code{species} column has the species-rank name of the NCBI
taxon. Thus a subspecies gets the name of its species, and a record that
is identified only to genus gets \code{NA}. Names in open nomenclature, for
example "Tityus sp. 1" and "Tityus sp. 2", stay different species. The
\code{organism} column always has the name as it is in the record.

If the NCBI Taxonomy data are not available, the function gets the genus and species from the organism name. It also gets family
(\code{-idae}), subfamily (\code{-inae}), and tribe (\code{-ini}) from the lineage text.
This method is correct for most animal names.
}

\subsection{Fill empty ranks}{

NCBI Taxonomy often has no tribe or subfamily for invertebrates. Use the
\code{ranks} argument to get these ranks from a second database. The function
does these steps:
\enumerate{
\item It finds the records that have a genus and one or more empty ranks.
\item It looks up each genus one time in the database.
\item It compares the lookup result with the ranks that the record already
has (family, order, class, and phylum). If one of them is different,
the genus name possibly belongs to a different group (a homonym). Then
the function does not change the record.
\item It fills only the empty ranks. It does not replace a rank that
exists.
}

The function saves the lookup results in \code{dir}, in the file
\verb{ranks_<database>.csv}. When you run the function again, it uses the saved
results and looks up only new genera. To look up all genera again, remove
the file.

Different databases can use different classifications. Use the
\code{filled_ranks} column to find the ranks that came from the second
database.
}

\subsection{Duplicates}{

A record can be in more than one download folder. The function keeps one
copy of each record and each feature.
}
